The widely studied concept of a maximum-weight Tai matching between a pair of rooted labeled trees consists of a one-to-node mapping that maintains ancestral links; however, for unordered trees with maximum-weight anti-Tai mappings, it is NP-hard. Another type is "anti-Tai mappings" where each node maps to a set of nodes such that ancestral relationships are inherited. This research demonstrates that finding "maximum-weighted anti-Tai mappings" (finding the heaviest such mapping) is also NP-complete, but when an upper bound $k$ exists for how many times a single node in the first tree can map to the second (a $k$-branching restriction), this problem becomes solvable via dynamic programming. Thus, if $k$ is treated as a fixed parameter and appears in the exponent, the weight of the heaviest mapping can be found in time $O(k! \log n)$—where $n$ represents the average node count—and this specific time frame applies to the weight calculation itself. Additionally, constructing (realizing) the actual mapping takes time exponential relative to $k$ but linear in $n$, expressed as $O(k! \log n]$. The notation used here is $O(k! \log n]$.

